WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.100
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.389
Whenever we look at these amazing research
papers on physical simulations, it is always

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:14.330
a joy seeing people discussing them in the
comments section.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:19.550
However, one thing that caught my attention
is that some people comment about how things

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.340
look, and not on how things move in these
papers.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.830
Which is fair enough, and to this end, I will
devote this episode to talk a little about

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.560
a few amazing techniques used in light transport
simulations.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.640
But first things first: when talking about
physics simulations, we are talking about

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.829
a technique that computes how things move.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:44.649
Then, we typically run a light simulation
program that computes how things look.

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:49.280
The two are completely independent, which
means that it is possible that the physical

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:55.120
behavior of bread breaking here is correct,
but the bread itself does not look perfectly

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.280
realistic.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.500
This second part depends on the quality of
the light simulation and the materials used

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:02.500
there.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.040
We can create such an image by simulating
the path of millions and millions of light

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.040
rays.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.220
And initially, this image will look noisy,
and as we add more and more rays, this image

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.470
will slowly clean up over time.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:21.470
If we don’t have a well-optimized program,
this can take from hours to days to compute.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:26.620
We can speed up this process by carefully
choosing where to shoot these rays, and this

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.130
is a technique that is called importance sampling.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:36.340
But then, around 1993, an amazing paper appeared
by the name Bidirectional Path Tracing, that

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:42.299
proposed that we don’t just start building
light paths from one direction, but two instead

00:01:42.299 --> 00:01:47.930
- one from the camera, and one from a light
source and then, connect them.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:52.390
This significantly improved the efficiency
of these light simulations, however, it opened

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:54.430
up a new can of worms.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:58.950
There are many different ways of connecting
these paths which leads to mathematical difficulties.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:05.120
For instance, we have to specify the probability
of a light path forming, but what do we do

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.450
if there are multiple ways of producing this
light path?

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.070
There will be multiple probabilities.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.340
What do we do with all this stuff?

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:19.081
To address this, Eric Veach described a magical
algorithm in this thesis, and thus, multiple

00:02:19.081 --> 00:02:21.250
importance sampling was born.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:26.320
I can say without exaggeration that this is
one of the most powerful techniques in all

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.640
photorealistic rendering research.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.790
What multiple importance sampling, or from
now on, MIS in short does, is combine these

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:39.860
multiple sampling techniques in a way that
accentuates the strengths of each of them.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:45.370
For instance, you can see the image created
by one sampling technique here, and the image

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:48.230
from a different one here.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:54.540
Both of them are quite noisy, but if we combine
them with MIS, we get this instead in the

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.250
same amount of time.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.380
A much smoother, less noisy image.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:05.150
In many cases, this can truly bring down the
computation times from several hours to several

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:06.400
minutes.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:07.650
Absolute witchcraft.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:12.890
Later, even more advanced techniques appeared
to accelerate the speed of these light simulation

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:13.890
programs.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:18.930
For instance, it is now not only possible
to compute light transport between points

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:23.310
in space, but between a point and a beam instead.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:27.450
You see the evolution of an image using this
photon beam-based technique.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.250
This way, we can get rid of the point-based
noise and get a much, much more appealing

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.900
rendering process.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:39.630
The lead author of this beam paper is Wojciech
Jarosz, who, three years later, ended up being

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:44.120
the head of the Rendering group at the amazing
Disney Reseach lab.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.560
Around that time he also hired me to work
with him at Disney on a project I can’t

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.800
talk about, which was an incredible and life-changing
experience and I will be forever grateful

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:54.800
for his kindness.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.530
By the way, he is now a professor at the Darthmouth
University and just keeps pumping out one

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.709
killer paper after another.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:07.910
So, as you might have guessed, if it is possible
to compute light transport between two points,

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:13.810
a point and a beam, later, it became possible
to do this between two beams.

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:17.340
None of these are for the faint of the heart,
but it works really well.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.799
But, there is a huge problem.

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:25.190
These techniques work with different dimensionalities,
or in other words, they estimate the final

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:30.680
result so differently, that they cannot be
combined with multiple importance sampling.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:36.470
That is indeed a problem, because all of these
have completely different strengths and weaknesses.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:41.139
And now, hold on to your papers, because we
have finally arrived to the main paper of

00:04:41.139 --> 00:04:42.290
this episode.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:49.050
It bears the name UPBP, which stands for unifying
points, beams and paths, and it formulates

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:54.490
multiple importance sampling between all of
these different kinds of light transport simulations.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:59.480
Basically, what we can do with this is throw
every advanced simulation program we can think

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:05.590
of together, and out comes a super powerful
version of them that combines all their strengths

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:09.010
and nullifies nearly all of their weaknesses.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.580
It is absolutely unreal.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.660
Here you see four completely different algorithms
running, and as you can see, they are noisy

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.389
and smooth at very different places.

00:05:19.389 --> 00:05:23.310
They are good at computing different kinds
of light transport.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:30.169
And now, hold on to your papers, because the
final result with the UPBP technique is this.

00:05:30.169 --> 00:05:31.440
Wow!

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:32.990
Light transport on steroids.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:37.889
While we look at some more results, I will
note that in my opinion, this is one of the

00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:41.290
best papers ever written in light transport
research.

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:45.270
The crazy thing is that I hardly ever hear
anybody talk about it.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:49.490
If any paper would deserve a bit more attention,
it is this one, so I hope this video will

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:51.090
help with that.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:56.150
I would like to dedicate this video to Jaroslav
Krivanek, the first author of this absolutely

00:05:56.150 --> 00:06:00.090
amazing paper, who has tragically passed away
a few months ago.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:06.050
In my memories, I think of him as the True
King of Multiple Importance Sampling and I

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.910
hope that now, you do too.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:14.050
Note that MIS is not limited to light transport
algorithms, it is a general concept that can

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:19.550
be used together with a mathematical technique
called Monte Carlo integration, which is used

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:25.040
pretty much everywhere, from finding out what
an electromagnetic field looks like, to financial

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.660
modeling, and much, much more.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:32.860
If you have anything to do with Monte Carlo
integration, please read Eric Veach’s thesis

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:38.200
and this paper, and if you feel that it is
a good fit, try to incorporate Multiple Importance

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:39.970
Sampling into your system.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:41.510
You’ll be glad you did.

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:46.630
Also, we have recorded my lectures of a Master-level
course on light transport simulations at the

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:48.760
Technical University of Vienna.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:53.080
In this course, we write write such a light
simulation program from scratch, and it is

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:57.490
available free of charge for everyone, no
strings attached, so make sure to click the

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:00.150
link in the video description to get started.

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:05.310
Additionally, I have implemented a small 1
dimensional example of MIS, if you wish to

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:08.880
pick it up and try it, that’s also available
in the video description.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.090
While talking about the Technical University
of Vienna - we are hiring for a PhD and a

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:15.560
PostDoc position.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:19.919
The call here about “Lighting Simulation
For Architectural Design” is advised by

00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:24.230
my PhD advisor, Michael Wimmer, who I highly
recommend.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:28.070
Apply now if you feel qualified, the link
is in the video description.

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:31.550
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

